  {
    "episode_id": 74127,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74127.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 26,
    "turn_text": "Back in those days, I was working you know, in my medical practice very hard. And if you remember, Todd, we would open at six o'clock in the morning. We'd work until six o'clock at night. We just worked. It was a rural hospital, rural community clinic. So there was a big need. We just wanted to provide that need for the people of the community. But because of the way I structured my life every night, about seven o'clock, I would get home. And Diane, my wonderful wife, would have dinner, or what we call in the South, supper ready. And at that time we would usually turn to one of the cables used as banks, whether it was Fox or CNN or whatever, and just watch what was going on, or what had happened that particular day. And we started noticing that things just kept, in our opinion, started to get in a little worse, a little worse. And we were at the point of our lives that if we were going to do something politically, now was the time we had to do it. So the decision was made that we would run for the fifth congressional district. We were fortunate to win the district. We ran hard, ran a good campaign. But we ran because we thought the country needed to go in a little bit of a different direction at that point.",
